Our bot is based on a goal-based and utility-based, model-based agent architecture.
The central agent function is the method choose_move() of the class VoronoiAgent. It is called via the function move() and decides, based on the current game state (game_state), which move to make.
The bot uses an internal world model that is built from the game state it receives (board, you, enemy, etc.). Based on this model, the bot chooses a situation-dependent goal strategy: looking for food (food_hunter), attacking the opponent (aggressive) or surviving neutrally (neutral).
To determine the most suitable goal behaviour and to evaluate the possible moves, the bot uses evaluation functions: the free space is estimated with a flood-fill algorithm (flood_fill()), and the function compute_voronoi() analyses the control of space compared to the opponent. This way every possible move receives a utility score, which is why the agent can be classified as utility-based.

The decision is not based only on the current percept, but also on an internal representation of the environment. Therefore it is not a simple reflex agent, but a model-based agent with strategic goal pursuit.
We chose this model because it can react flexibly to different game situations and allows a dynamic evaluation. By combining goal pursuit and utility maximisation, the agent is able to act both offensively and defensively, which is a major advantage in a competitive environment like Battlesnake.
-----------
In addition to the basic concepts of a goal-based and utility-based agent, we integrated further considerations and strategies into our bot. They help us to avoid risks, increase flexibility and use space control strategically.

Risk avoidance
For every potential move, our bot checks whether the target cell
•	is inside the board,
•	is occupied by a snake (our own tail counts as free, unless food is eaten in this move),
•	leads into a dead end (detect_dead_end()). Such a move is only discarded if our own tail cannot be reached from there either (is_tail_reachable()).
Moves that pass these checks are considered safe moves. If only one safe move is left, it is executed directly. If there is no safe move left, the bot chooses a random direction.

Dynamic mode selection
Our bot uses a simple rule-based model to switch its strategic behaviour:
•	food_hunter, if its health is below 40 or our snake is not longer than the opponent
•	aggressive, if our snake is at least 2 cells longer than the opponent, its health is at least 50 and, according to the Voronoi calculation, the bot controls at least as much space as the opponent
•	neutral in all other cases

Move evaluation per mode
•	food_hunter: proximity to the nearest food (closest_food_distance()) plus free space from the flood fill
•	aggressive: own free space (weighted twice) minus the opponent's space (weighted three times), plus the Voronoi advantage after the move; a move next to the head of a shorter opponent is rewarded with +1000
•	neutral: free space from the flood fill plus the current Voronoi advantage
Finally, the move with the highest score is executed.

Space control with a Voronoi strategy
The function compute_voronoi() divides the board into areas of influence using a simultaneous breadth-first search from both heads. Cells reached by both snakes count as neutral. This information influences both the mode selection and the evaluation of possible moves. Space means survival time: whoever controls more space can dodge more flexibly, reach food or trap the opponent. The Voronoi calculation provides an effective and dynamic assessment of the bot's own position.

Flood fill to estimate freedom of movement
Our bot uses flood_fill() to calculate, for every potential move, the number of reachable cells (limited to 50) and their quality (number of free neighbouring cells), in order to avoid tight spaces. This estimate helps to judge whether a move leads into a position that is dangerous in the long run. This way, space is used as a strategic advantage.

Note: The bot is designed for duels against exactly one opponent (1 vs 1).
